common='''The frozen input is a forced state at beta=3.0952, lambda=0.989973, K=24, with1332 active coefficients. Its SHA256 is e3a4cb7cb81f32a815689a5a53d7d512bee3a3f437ea1881eabef10b9cfe07d3; the frozen Jacobian SHA256 is b344029032af8429ec5d79486d3bf4c8a0abcaa108a585a4898b43ed320a0b2d. The two selected directions are the weakest coefficient singular vector of J_h-I and the independently computed weakest vector in the full-plane G norm integral (r^(1/4)+r^(-1/4))|G|^2 dX. Each input vector has unit physical norm. The coefficient strain projection is applied before the relative full-plane inverse. There is no added harmonic strain or independent drift.\n\nNUM first fits transported temperature, differentiates that fit in the vorticity integral, and then fits G. Our map transports the raw temperature gradient with an independent DOP853 characteristic solver. NUM also uses higher boundary jets and fixed time steps; ours uses leading jets and an exact-radius event with a compatible gradient. Thus reported gaps compare different numerical procedures, not two implementations of an identical finite-dimensional map. They do not by themselves establish an error in the saved Jacobian of NUM's own map.\n\nAll ratios use max of the absolute values of the two compared actions, without an artificial floor. The recorded zero-denominator flags are retained. No compared value in the table has a zero denominator. Finite variant spreads, quadrature error estimates, and agreement of methods are not rigorous error bounds. No operator-norm enclosure, exact-map singular-value bound, nonlinear root, branch, fold, unforced profile or full B2 endpoint is established.\n'''
