(paths[213]/'verification/summary_metrics.json').write_text(json.dumps(summary_metrics,indent=2)+'\n')
for k,p in paths.items():
 j=a[k];timing=f"Actual computation: {j['started_utc']} to {j['ended_utc']}; elapsed {j['elapsed_seconds']:.6f}s; peak RSS {j['max_rss_kib']}KiB; {len(j['checks'])}/{len(j['checks'])} controls passed. One single-thread nice19 CPU process; no GPU or NUM module execution.\n"
 if k==210:
  body='''P210 used an adaptive full-plane modal inverse with a once-subtracted first mode, controlled at three points by independent Cartesian Duffy quadrature. It recomputed both direction inverses on477 radial nodes, used a C1 velocity interpolant, and evaluated four-point parameter differences over five cutoff/grid/step/tolerance variants at five physical points. All18 algebra/manufactured controls passed. The old P200 base cache had183 quadrature warnings; these were retained. The direction quadratures succeeded. P212 replaces the base with a moment-stable cache and improves inverse controls and differentiation; use its refined results below rather than treating P210's spread as an error budget.\n'''
 elif k==212:
  body='''P212 retained the exact second-mode moment of each binary coefficient vector as a rational value, including its tiny residual after strain projection. It used P202's separately integrated moment-stable base cache, recomputed both direction inverses, and constructed a C2 velocity from a degree-seven C3 stream interpolant on477 log-radius nodes. Analytic moving-hit sensitivities were compared with four-point parameter differences. Fifty analytic rows (five points, two directions, five cutoff/grid/tolerance variants) and thirty parameter-difference rows completed. Fourteen mode/moment controls passed. The second-mode identity changes no canonical inverse or gauge.\n\nAt Cartesian orders24 and36, the maximum direction-velocity component discrepancies were respectively3.372012336e-9 and3.242064672e-10 for the coefficient-weak vector, and1.868176697e-9 and1.820590345e-10 for the physical-weak vector. Both sets of477 direction-node integrations reported success; P202's base cache had1908 successful subintegrals. Sampled fine-grid interpolation and source errors are in P213 summary_metrics.json. These are sampling diagnostics, not enclosures.\n\nThe cutoff values were .003,.001,.0003; a coarser interpolation grid and rtol1e-12 were separate controls. P213 tightens rtol to3e-14 and uses larger finite-difference steps to reduce cancellation. The table below uses that refinement.\n'''
 else:
  body='''P213 checked the exact saved base coefficients, beta and lambda; the independent strain row; saved J_h actions; and the identity relating J_h v and J_h Dv. All10 controls passed. It compared the raw base output with the actual saved fitted output x_star+f0, not merely the stored input. At ten point/direction pairs it computed analytic derivatives at rtol1e-12 and3e-14 and four-point differences at h=.01,.003,.001. It also checked the analytic response to the sum of the two unscaled directions.\n\nThe maximum Omega linearity discrepancy was5.649059345e-12 (relative7.244988517e-10), and the largest discrepancy over all four transported outputs was8.789214148e-11. At rtol3e-14, h=.01, the largest FD/analytic Omega discrepancy was7.434737485e-9; smaller steps could increase noise. The maximum tangency defect n.dot(S) was%.6g. These confirm numerical consistency of the stated finite-cutoff implementation; they do not certify its true error.\n\nThe raw and fitted base outputs already differ:\n\n'''%summary_metrics['analytic_max_normal_hit_derivative_error']+btable+'''\nThe derivative decomposition in PROOF.md is exact under fixed fit operators, sufficient mixed regularity and a non-grazing transverse first hit. With raw M=B+Kt and fitted N=Q_G[B+KQ_F t],\n\n dot(N)-dot(M)=(Q_G-I)dot(M)+Q_G[(dot(K))(Q_F-I)t+K(Q_F-I)dot(t)].\n\nBoundary-jet and time/inverse discretization differences supply additional terms in the actual numerical comparison. None is independently bounded here.\n'''
